\section{Exposure Bias Scaling}\label{sec:appendix-eb-scaling}

Figure~\ref{fig:eb-scaling} quantifies two dimensions of the exposure bias phenomenon. Panel~(a) compares the TF/AR gap ratio across all training variants and both architectures. On Qwen, ThreadGate achieves the smallest gap among standard variants (7.8$\times$) while PCGrad shows the largest (16.7$\times$, consistent with its counterproductive effect on AR GAR). Scheduled sampling reduces the gap to ${\sim}$1.3$\times$, the only variant approaching parity. On Llama, both ThreadGate and LoRA-only show $\sim$27$\times$ gaps (by median), larger than any Qwen variant. Panel~(b) shows that the gap compounds with conversation length: at 3.6 mean turns ($N$\,=\,50), ThreadGate's gap is 8$\times$; at 10 turns ($N$\,=\,5), it widens to 31$\times$. This length dependence is consistent with cumulative distributional drift---each model-generated turn introduces additional divergence from the teacher-forced training distribution, and by turn~10 the accumulated drift overwhelms any gating signal.

\begin{figure*}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\textwidth]{fig_eb_scaling.pdf}
\caption{Exposure bias scaling analysis. (a)~TF/AR gap ratio across training variants; scheduled sampling (SS, ${\sim}$1.3$\times$) is the only variant near parity. Llama gaps ($\sim$27$\times$) exceed all Qwen variants. (b)~Gap compounds with conversation length: ThreadGate's gap widens from 8$\times$ (3.6 turns) to 31$\times$ (10 turns).}
\label{fig:eb-scaling}
\end{figure*}

\section{Per-Turn AR GAR Dynamics}\label{sec:appendix-ar-gar-dynamics}

To characterize how goal attention evolves across conversation turns under autoregressive generation, we compute per-turn AR GAR for all six Qwen-2.5-7B training configurations (Base, LoRA-only, Uniform Gate, PCGrad, Scheduled Sampling, ThreadGate) on the held-out evaluation set. Figure~\ref{fig:kl_ar_gar_curves} plots per-turn AR GAR trajectories across turns 1--5, and Figure~\ref{fig:kl_ar_gar_bar} compares mid-turn AR GAR levels across configurations.

All six configurations exhibit monotonic GAR decay as turn index increases, consistent with cumulative exposure bias eroding attention to goal tokens. The decay rate varies substantially: Scheduled Sampling retains by far the highest mid-turn GAR (0.423), followed distantly by ThreadGate (0.064), LoRA-only (0.037), Uniform Gate (0.033), PCGrad (0.030), and Base (0.019). Base decays most rapidly, losing the majority of its initial GAR within the first few turns. The large SS--TG gap mirrors Table~\ref{tab:four-way} and confirms that distributional robustification---not gating architecture alone---is the primary driver of AR GAR retention.\footnote{SS configuration uses seed 123 data; all other configurations use seed 42. Cross-seed trends are consistent (\S\ref{subsec:ss}).}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_kl_ar_gar_curves.pdf}
\caption{Per-turn AR GAR trajectories for six Qwen-2.5-7B configurations (turns 1--5). All models show monotonic decay; Scheduled Sampling retains far higher GAR throughout (0.53$\to$0.39), while the remaining five variants cluster below 0.13 and decay rapidly.}
\label{fig:kl_ar_gar_curves}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_kl_ar_gar_bar.pdf}
\caption{Mid-turn AR GAR comparison across six training configurations. Ranking from highest to lowest: Scheduled Sampling (0.423), ThreadGate (0.064), LoRA-only (0.037), Uniform Gate (0.033), PCGrad (0.030), Base (0.019).}
\label{fig:kl_ar_gar_bar}
\end{figure}

This pattern contrasts with teacher-forced evaluation, where GAR remains relatively stable across turns for all configurations. Under teacher forcing, the model conditions on ground-truth context at each turn, preventing the distributional drift that causes GAR erosion in AR mode. The divergence between TF stability and AR decay provides turn-level evidence for the exposure bias mechanism described in \S\ref{sec:analysis}: training-time attention patterns are optimized for a distribution that AR generation progressively departs from.

